import java.util.Stack;

class Solution {
    public boolean isValid(String s) {

        // Create a stack to store opening brackets
        Stack<Character> stack = new Stack<>();

        // Convert the string into a character array and iterate through each character
        for(char ch : s.toCharArray()){

            // If the character is an opening bracket
            if(ch == '(' || ch == '{' || ch == '['){

                // Push the opening bracket onto the stack
                stack.push(ch);
            } 
            else{

                // If we encounter a closing bracket but the stack is empty
                // it means there is no matching opening bracket
                if(stack.isEmpty()) 
                    return false;

                // Pop the top element from the stack
                // This should be the corresponding opening bracket
                char top = stack.pop();

                // Check if the popped opening bracket matches the closing bracket
                // If not, the parentheses are invalid
                if((ch == ')' && top != '(') ||
                   (ch == '}' && top != '{') ||
                   (ch == ']' && top != '[')){
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        // After processing all characters:
        // If the stack is empty → all brackets matched correctly
        // If not empty → some opening brackets were never closed
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }
}
💡 How the Algorithm Works

Push opening brackets ( { [ into the stack.

When a closing bracket appears:

Check if the stack is empty → invalid.

Pop the top element.

Verify if it matches the closing bracket.

After finishing the loop:

If stack is empty → valid.

If stack is not empty → invalid.

Example

Input:

s = "{[]}"

Stack operations:

Character	Stack
{	{
[	{ [
]	{
}	empty

Result → true

Complexity

Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity: O(n) (stack may store all opening brackets)
